{"name":"Silver White","hex":"#e7e8e3","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"silver white","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Synonym of white lead, particularly  in the form used in 19th-century Parisian paints."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/silver_white"}],"entries":[{"title":"Silver","blocks":[{"text":"Silver is a chemical element; it has symbol Ag and atomic number 47. A soft, white, lustrous transition metal, it exhibits the highest electrical conductivity, thermal conductivity and reflectivity of any metal. Silver is found in the Earth's crust in the pure, free elemental form, as an alloy with gold (electrum) and other metals, and in minerals such as acanthite, argentite and chlorargyrite. Most silver is recovered as a byproduct of lead–zinc, copper and gold mining and refining."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Silver_crystal.jpg/330px-Silver_crystal.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silver"},{"title":"White","blocks":[{"text":"White is the lightest color and is achromatic. It is the color of objects such as snow, chalk, and milk, and is the opposite of black. White objects fully reflect and scatter all the visible wavelengths of light. White on television and computer screens is created by a mixture of red, blue, and green light. The color white can be given with white pigments, especially titanium dioxide."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg/330px-Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White"},{"title":"Silver (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Silver or metallic gray is a color tone resembling gray that is a representation of the color of polished silver."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Clemens_Friedell_silver_centerpiece_bowl_top_view_-_HLABG.png/330px-Clemens_Friedell_silver_centerpiece_bowl_top_view_-_HLABG.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silver_(color)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/1000oz.silver.bullion.bar.top.jpg/500px-1000oz.silver.bullion.bar.top.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/1000oz.silver.bullion.bar.top.jpg/500px-1000oz.silver.bullion.bar.top.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Silver bullion bar, 1000 ounces","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A1000oz.silver.bullion.bar.top.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/Ag_atomic_wire.jpg/500px-Ag_atomic_wire.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/Ag_atomic_wire.jpg/500px-Ag_atomic_wire.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Silver is extremely ductile and, like gold, can be drawn into a wire one atom wide.[14]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAg_atomic_wire.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/80/Sulfid_st%C5%99%C3%ADbrn%C3%BD.PNG/500px-Sulfid_st%C5%99%C3%ADbrn%C3%BD.PNG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/80/Sulfid_st%C5%99%C3%ADbrn%C3%BD.PNG/500px-Sulfid_st%C5%99%C3%ADbrn%C3%BD.PNG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Silver(I) sulfide","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASulfid_st%C5%99%C3%ADbrn%C3%BD.PNG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/f/f4/Common_Silver_Halide_Precipitates.jpg/500px-Common_Silver_Halide_Precipitates.jpg?utm_source